{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3219","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3219","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3219\",\"parent_revision\":\"c3b75687e5c3c37c8d9179725ea0674609e2cc9c9585cf4ce3ffa5ac7f9efccf\",\"root_revision\":\"c3b75687e5c3c37c8d9179725ea0674609e2cc9c9585cf4ce3ffa5ac7f9efccf\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-24\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe rule governing GitHub auto-closing keywords in dh commits and PR bodies is stated in 12 places across the development-harness plugin, three of which now contradict the others, and it is framed as a prohibition rather than as the convention to follow.\\n\\nMeasured state (primary checkout, excluding .claude/worktrees): 30 mentions of `Fixes #N` / `Closes #N` / `Resolves #N` across 17 files. Classified:\\n\\n- 1 authoritative statement: skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/commit-conventions.md\\n- 1 permitted use site: skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md:732 (appends `Fixes #NNN` to the final commit)\\n- 3 sites that grep for the trailer to detect an existing PR: skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md:163, .../work/issue-first.md:50, docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md:752 and :782\\n- 9 pure restatements of the prohibition: agents/swarm-task-planner.md:538 and :602, skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md:258, skills/clear-cove-task-design/SKILL.md:179, skills/development-harness/SKILL.md:75, skills/generate-task/SKILL.md:87 and :111, skills/start-task/SKILL.md:179 and :181, skills/task-decomposition/SKILL.md:243, .../work/plan.md:77, .../work/post-planning.md:40\\n- 3 sites that contradict the rule: close/start.md:166 states \\\"The PR body contains `Fixes #N` \u2014 the issue will auto-close on merge\\\", directly against commit-conventions.md:7 which forbids it; .claude/skills/gh/references/issue-stories.md:67 teaches `PR body: \\\"Closes #N\\\"` as the workflow; plugins/conventional-commits/README.md:38 lists `Fixes #N` as an ordinary optional footer.\\n\\nImpact \u2014 the prohibition does not hold. Two PRs merged in the seven days to 2026-08-24 emitted a banned trailer despite the nine prohibition sites: PR #3174 wrote `Closes #3162`, PR #3199 wrote `close #3145`. Across the same window 115 PRs merged and only 16 declared any closing reference, so a PR generally carries no trace of which issue it serves, and closure depends entirely on /dh:complete-implementation running.\\n\\nImpact \u2014 the trailer is redundant where it is permitted. complete-implementation applies `status:verified` (SKILL.md:678), then the Final Step appends `Fixes #NNN` (:732), then \\\"Resolve the Issue\\\" calls `backlog resolve --selector` (:764). The resolve call already closes the item; the trailer adds a second closure path that fires on merge, which misbehaves when more than one PR is needed for an issue.\\n\\nImpact \u2014 the guard the spread was built to provide is the right goal. The dh workflow (work-backlog-item to implement-feature to complete-implementation) deliberately guards against an issue closing on PR merge before assessment, review, and label updates have run. That intent must survive this change.\\n\\nRepository-instruction conflict: the prohibition framing is contrary to mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents, which states that steering by prohibition drags the forbidden behaviour into context and makes it more available, and that the fix is to state the positive target so the banned token is never spoken. It is also contrary to the single-source-of-truth requirement in the same guide and to AGENTS.md's DRY expectations for plugin content.\\n\\nScope note: this is plugin content shipped to consumers (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode), so the policy belongs in the development-harness plugin, not in .claude/rules/, which loads only in this checkout and only under Claude Code. Per plugin-creator:shared-content-references, prose shared by two or more components in a plugin belongs in the plugin-root docs/ directory rather than inside one skill's references/.\\n\\nUser-provided context: the repo owner has specified the convention to adopt. A single dh policy should state that when the dh plugin is used to groom and implement a GitHub or GitLab issue, commits and PR bodies use a `Relates to: #N` convention instead of an auto-closing keyword, so the issue is not closed when the PR merges, multiple PRs for one issue remain possible, and the assessment and review processes in /dh:complete-implementation are what close the issue. The owner also supplied the mechanism: GitHub's auto-closing keywords are Close, Closes, Closed, Fix, Fixes, Fixed, Resolve, Resolves, Resolved followed by #N, which close the issue on merge to the default branch; any other phrasing that mentions #N links without closing, including `Refs #N`, `References #N`, `Related to #N`, `See #N`, and `Part of #N`.\\n\\nPoints needing design decisions during grooming, not pre-decided here: whether the nine restatement sites get a pointer to the policy or are deleted outright; whether complete-implementation's final commit keeps a trailer at all given the redundant resolve call that follows it; and how the three grep-for-the-trailer detection sites should be rewritten, since a git log search for `Fixes #N` will stop matching once task commits carry `Relates to: #N`.</description>\\n</invoke>\\n\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session audit of merged-PR issue linkage, 2026-08-24\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-24\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Scope Correction The Policy Is Conditional On The Development Path\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe repo owner has scoped the convention. It is not repo-wide:\\n\\n- PRs produced through the full dh workflow (`/dh:work-backlog-item` to `/dh:implement-feature` to\\n  `/dh:complete-implementation`) use `Relates to: #N`. That path is rigorous \u2014 grooming, quality\\n  gates, assessment, review, label updates \u2014 and its issue closure belongs to\\n  `/dh:complete-implementation`, not to a merge trailer. A trailer there would close the issue\\n  before those gates ran, and would make a second PR for the same issue impossible.\\n- PRs produced outside that loop, in human-supervised development, may use either `Fixes #N` or\\n  `Relates to: #N`. The author chooses based on whether that single PR completes the issue.\\n\\nSo the policy is a conditional keyed on which path produced the PR, not a blanket prohibition. This\\nchanges the finding classification recorded above.\\n\\n### Corrected finding set\\n\\nOf the 30 mentions across 17 files, 27 are development-harness plugin content and 3 are not.\\nRe-classified against the conditional policy:\\n\\nGenuine contradictions inside the dh path \u2014 2, not the 3 originally recorded:\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md:166`\\n  states \\\"The PR body contains `Fixes #N` \u2014 the issue will auto-close on merge\\\" and treats that as\\n  the expected state. This is dh workflow content, so it contradicts the dh convention directly.\\n- `.claude/skills/gh/references/issue-stories.md:67` prescribes `PR body: \\\"Closes #N\\\"` inside a\\n  lifecycle that uses dh's own label vocabulary (`status:needs-grooming`, `status:in-progress`).\\n  It documents the dh flow while prescribing the keyword the dh flow forbids. It lives in a\\n  repo-root skill rather than the plugin, so grooming has to decide whether to scope the lifecycle\\n  it describes to the non-dh path or correct the keyword.\\n\\nReclassified as correct, no change needed \u2014 1:\\n\\n- `plugins/conventional-commits/README.md:38` lists `Fixes #N` as an optional commit footer in a\\n  generic conventional-commits reference. That plugin is not dh content and its readers are not on\\n  the dh path, so the entry is accurate under the conditional policy. It was wrongly recorded above\\n  as a contradiction.\\n\\nNewly identified, wrong in the opposite direction \u2014 1:\\n\\n- `.claude/rules/fix-delegation-discipline.md:27` bans the trailer for every fix delegation. The\\n  rule's own scope line reads \\\"applies to all agent types (subagent, kage-bunshin, teammate)\\\",\\n  and its Commit node carries the parenthetical \\\"(quality gates handle issue closure)\\\" \u2014 a dh\\n  assumption embedded in a repo-wide rule. Under the conditional policy a human-supervised fix\\n  outside the dh loop may use `Fixes #N`, so this rule over-applies the dh constraint to work the\\n  policy permits. It needs the condition stated, not the ban removed.\\n\\nThe 9 restatement sites and the 3 grep-for-the-trailer detection sites are all development-harness\\nplugin content, so their classification above is unchanged by this correction.\\n\\n### Consequence for the policy's wording\\n\\nA single unconditional statement will not serve. The policy has to name the path it governs, and\\nsay what applies outside it \u2014 otherwise it reproduces the over-application now present in\\n`fix-delegation-discipline.md`. Stating only the dh half leaves a reader outside the dh loop with a\\nprohibition that does not apply to them, which is how that rule reached its current state.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Resolved Question The Trailer At Complete Implementation 732 Should Be Removed\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRecorded above as an open design decision. It is now settled by direct read of the skill and the\\nbackend implementation. Replace it with `Relates to: #NNN` or omit the reference entirely; do not\\nkeep an auto-closing keyword there.\\n\\nExecution order inside `/dh:complete-implementation`:\\n\\n1. `Apply status:verified Label` (SKILL.md:678) \u2014 applies the label through the backend.\\n2. `Final Step: Commit and Push Remaining Changes` (:732) \u2014 appends `Fixes #NNN` to the commit body\\n   and pushes. The push goes to a working branch, not the default branch.\\n3. `Team Shutdown` (:740).\\n4. `Resolve the Issue` (:764) \u2014 `backlog resolve --selector {item_ref} --summary \\\"Implementation\\n   complete \u2014 AC verified PASS\\\"`.\\n\\nWhat step 4 actually does, read from `backlog_core/gh_client.py:1319` (`resolve_github_issue`,\\ndocstring \\\"Close GitHub issue as completed with structured evidence trail. ADR-9.\\\"): it posts a\\n`## Resolved` comment assembled from summary, method, notes, follow-ups and findings, then calls\\n`_update_issue_graphql(..., state=\\\"CLOSED\\\")`. The issue is closed immediately at step 4, with an\\nevidence trail, before any PR merges.\\n\\nFour reasons the trailer at step 2 should go:\\n\\n1. Redundant on the success path. The issue is already CLOSED by step 4. The trailer lives in a\\n   commit on a branch, so it can only act later, on merge to the default branch \u2014 attempting to\\n   close an issue that is already closed. Its sole effect is a no-op.\\n2. Actively harmful on the interrupted path, and that path is documented. Both\\n   `docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md:686` and\\n   `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/post-planning.md:40` state that\\n   `/work-backlog-item resolve` exists for when \\\"complete-implementation was interrupted before the\\n   resolve step\\\". The trailer is written at step 2; resolve is step 4. An interruption between them\\n   leaves a pushed commit carrying `Fixes #NNN` against an unresolved issue. When that branch\\n   merges, GitHub closes the issue with no resolution comment, no evidence trail, and without the\\n   `status:verified` label having been confirmed \u2014 the exact premature closure the nine prohibition\\n   sites exist to prevent, reintroduced at the one site permitted to write the keyword.\\n3. Harmful on reopen. If the issue is reopened after step 4 because review found the work\\n   incomplete or a follow-up is needed, merging that branch re-closes it silently through the\\n   trailer, outside every gate. This is the multi-PR case the `Relates to: #N` convention is meant\\n   to keep open.\\n4. Not portable. The instruction itself says \\\"omit for beads IDs\\\", so the trailer is a\\n   GitHub-only closure mechanism. The `backlog resolve` path works across every configured backend.\\n\\nThe structural defect underneath all four: step 2 writes a closure instruction that only step 4\\nearns the authority to issue. Closure is encoded into an artifact before the gate that authorises\\nit has run, and once that artifact is pushed the encoding survives the interruption of the gate.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"593d2c98d12e0338ddce524ec759bcb6258b16f90c12ddeb330297b5ac20be9e\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQVpvfg\"}"}